  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 301 AM CDT Tue Aug 18 2026\n\nThe initial round of showers and storms that was expected as\nthis mid level shortwave coupled with a surface front push \nthrough the area, and round two is the cluster of showers and \nstorms over eastern South Dakota. This second round is expected \nto move across southern Minnesota this morning, arriving in our \ncoverage area closer to 5-6am and affecting the area along and \nsouth of the Minnesota River, with the best chance for more than\nshowers being on the southern edge of the line that is more \nlikely to be in Iowa. All this to say, we do not expect to see \nsignificant or widespread impacts from the second round, \nprimarily some showers and maybe rumbles of thunder that exits \nthe area later this morning. The CAMs all have some form of cold\nair northwest flow isolated showers lingering throughout the \nday, and with a bit of mixed layer CAPE we could see a few \nrumbles of thunder from any activity as well, although with \nunidirectional northwest flow, it is unlikely that anything more\nthan a rumble or two results from it. The front will fully pass\nthrough during the day and leave us with weak high pressure by \nthis time tomorrow morning, resulting in quiet weather through \nThursday when our next chance for showers and storms arrives. \nTemperatures today and tomorrow will be in the 70s thanks to the\nweak subsidence from the weak high pressure as well as the \nmeager CAA from the northwest flow, returning to the 80s on \nThursday as our next system arrives.\n\nSpeaking of, our next chance for rain will arrive on Thursday as\na broad area of weak forcing stretches south of a weak surface\nlow in central Canada, with northwesterly flow aloft \ncontinuing. The surface low looks to move southeast towards the \nnorthern tip of Lake Superior by 12z Friday, and as it does our \narea finds itself between the weak leading warm front and \nsagging cold front, resulting in chance for scattered showers \nand storms starting late Thursday morning and continuing until \nthe cold front moves through by midday Friday. The setup is \nrather interesting with a narrow upper level jet streak left \nexit region arriving Friday morning coupled with a low level \njet, with the low level jet also within northwest flow which is \na rather unique setup as typical nocturnal low level jet \nfeatures tend to favor southerly flow. One would think we will \nstruggle with moisture with this kind of setup, which is \ncertainly a consideration, however most of the guidance shows \ndew points in the low to mid 60s with the ECMWF even suggesting \nupper 60s by Thursday evening. As long as our low level \nadvection does not contain significant dry air, we should have \nenough moisture to force showers and storms. I would expect the \nforecast trend as we approach to gradually favor increasing PoPs\nand QPF, with ensemble probabilities showing above 80% \nlikelihood for at least a tenth of an inch across most of the \nMPX coverage area by 7am Friday. The percentages get lower than \n30% for a half inch or more, which seems realistic given the \npattern. As the cold front arrives on Friday, one last push of \nprecipitation will make way for more seasonal temperatures and \nweak high pressure to round out the 7 day period into early next\nweek. The longer range pattern continues to favor northwest \nflow aloft as the ridge slides westwards, giving us potential \nfor shortwaves to produce precipitation, however also favoring a\nrelatively low confidence forecast at this time scale.",
